ENTRY            C3136   20260717   20261014   20261014       C259
SUBENT        C3136001   20260717   20261014   20261014       C259
BIB                 11         42
TITLE      First measurement of the 20Ne(alpha,p)23Na reaction
           at energies relevant for type Ia supernovae
AUTHOR     (C.Boomershine,D.W.Bardayan,R.J.deBoer,P.D.O'Malley,
           S.R.Carmichael,L.Caves,A.Davis,A.Gula,K.Howard,
           R.Kelmar,A.Mitchell,L.Morales,S.Moylan,D.Robertson,
           E.Stech)
REFERENCE  (J,PR/C,113,065804,2026)
           #doi:10.1103/vkcb-8hzg
INSTITUTE  (1USANOT)
           (1USAUSA) Department of Physics and Astronomy,
           Swarthmore College, Swarthmore, Pennsylvania 19081, USA
           (1USAUSA) Department of Physics, University of Southern
            Indiana, Evansville, Indiana 47712, USA
FACILITY   (VDG,1USANOT) NSL 5U pelletron.
INC-SPECT  Typical beam currents ranged from 10 to 20 microA.
           The energy was stepped in 5- to 20-keV intervals
           depending on whether the cross section was expected
           to be rapidly or slowly changing based upon the
           energy dependence.
DETECTOR   (SIBAR) The surface barrier detectors were double-
           collimated with a series of slits and apertures to
           only view the center interaction region of the beam
           with the gas target.
SAMPLE     The Ne gas was injected at 10 Torr of pressure and
           was passed through a liquid nitrogen cold trap after
           being recirculated for reinjection into the system.
METHOD     For the measurement of the 20Ne(alpha,p)23Na and the
           20Ne(alpha,p1)23Na reaction cross sections, 4He
           beams in the q = 2+ charge state were accelerated to
           energies from 3.5 to 6 MeV. The beams were then used
           to bombard a natural Ne target created with the
           Rhinoceros extended gas target system. Six ruggedized
           100-microm-thick Si surface barrier detectors were
           installed at 45, 60, 90, 105, 120, and 135 degrees to
           detect charged particles emitted from induced
           reactions. Data from each silicon detector were
           compiled into histograms that typically contained
           four to six distinct peaks. These peaks corresponded
           to the various reactions that took place in the
           target chamber.
STATUS     (APRVD) Approved by D.W.Bardayan on July 31, 2026.
HISTORY    (20260717C) BP
ENDBIB              42
